# ---- abstract
R.append((r"""\noindent Suppose AI supplies codifiable cognition in abundance but not tacit local knowledge, the capacity to bear the consequences of error, or the work of turning a vague need into a well-specified task. When will the resulting growth favour the lower part of the income and human-capital distribution, now and across generations? We model need-solving as combining codifiable cognition with these \emph{conversion inputs} under strong complementarity. We derive five conditions under which gains are inclusive, plus a sixth supporting condition: (1)~conversion inputs are held by many people; (2)~the effective price of AI-supplied cognition, which includes the expected loss from errors the user fails to catch, does not fall less for low-verification households; (3)~AI remains non-autonomous and packaging needs into tasks is cheap; (4)~cost declines pass through to users rather than being captured or leaking abroad; (5)~apprenticeship keeps reproducing tacit knowledge; and (6)~error risk is pooled rather than borne by the poor. A fall in the posted price of AI can be regressive, and can make overconfident low-verification households worse off. Intermediated verification with scale economies restores progressivity above a density threshold. Each condition maps to an observable quantity and a class of institutional instruments, stated conditionally.""",
r"""\noindent Suppose AI supplies codifiable cognition in abundance but not tacit local knowledge, the capacity to bear the consequences of error, or the work of turning a vague need into a well-specified task. When does the resulting growth favour lower-income households, now and in the next cohort? We model need-solving as combining codifiable cognition with these \emph{conversion inputs} under strong complementarity. In one economy that combines factor prices and a task market, five conditions and a supporting one are jointly sufficient for relatively progressive gains and a non-falling share of the next cohort holding conversion inputs: these inputs are widely held; verification is intermediated by default; AI stays non-autonomous, packaging is cheap and knowledge does not fall with income; fees leave intermediaries above a density threshold; apprenticeship is subsidised and mentor capacity suffices; and error risk is pooled. Each condition can fail alone and break the result. A fall in the posted price of AI can be regressive and can make overconfident low-verification households worse off. Each condition maps to an observable and a class of instruments, stated conditionally."""))

# ---- introduction
R.append((r"Proposition~\ref{prop:synthesis} shows that the conditions are jointly sufficient for inclusive gains in the model. For each, it gives a parameterisation in which its failure alone breaks inclusiveness.",
r"Proposition~\ref{prop:synthesis} places the factor-price model of Proposition~\ref{prop:valueshift} and the task market of Proposition~\ref{prop:autonomy} in one economy, states the conditions precisely in that economy, and proves that they are jointly sufficient for inclusive gains there. For each condition it gives a parameterisation in which that condition alone fails and inclusiveness breaks; these parameterisations are constructed and checked numerically."))
R.append((r"An intermediary that verifies for many clients, with fixed costs and low marginal cost, restores relative progressivity above a density threshold that we derive. Under overconfidence, though, a voluntary market for verification under-supplies it. Restoring progressivity then requires that the intermediated channel be the default.",
r"An intermediary that verifies for many clients, with fixed costs and low marginal cost, restores relative progressivity when three conditions hold together: every household for which intermediation is efficient is served through it, the number of clients per intermediary exceeds a threshold that we derive, and the self-verifying households above the cutoff satisfy the gradient condition of Lemma~\ref{lem:schedule}. Density alone is not enough. Under overconfidence, a voluntary market can leave unserved some households for which intermediation is efficient. Whether it does depends on the fee and on how close their verification capacity is to the intermediary's; making the intermediated channel the default removes the problem."))
R.append((r"When AI makes entry tasks cheap, market apprenticeship falls below the social optimum. It does so precisely as the social value of each apprenticeship rises (Proposition~\ref{prop:apprentice}).",
r"When AI makes entry tasks cheap, market apprenticeship falls below the social optimum, provided the expert premium is positive, some supervision costs exceed the AI price, and mentors have enough capacity. The gap widens as AI cheapens entry tasks, because the external value of an apprenticeship, the discounted expert premium $\beta W$, rises (Proposition~\ref{prop:apprentice}). When mentor capacity binds, a fall in apprenticeship for one cohort reduces the number of mentors available to the next, so the fall persists or deepens across cohorts (Corollary~\ref{cor:capacity})."))
R.append((r"The third is the link between entry-task automation, the apprenticeship externality and the reproduction of the scarce complement across generations.",
r"The third is the link between entry-task automation, the apprenticeship externality and the reproduction of the scarce complement across generations, including the persistence that arises when mentor capacity binds."))

