# ---- Proposition 1(iv)
R.append((r"\item Hold $\tau(y)$ fixed. A household holding $\tau(y)$ units of $t$ gains $[w(p_c^1)-w(p_c^0)]\tau(y)$ from a strict price fall $p_c^1<p_c^0$; with the price of $S$ fixed, this is the exact equivalent variation. This gain relative to income is non-increasing in $y$ if and only if $\tau(y)/y$ is non-increasing.",
r"\item Let utility be quasilinear in a numeraire, so that changes in income are money-metric, and hold $\tau(y)\ge0$ fixed. A household holding $\tau(y)$ units of $t$ gains $[w(p_c^1)-w(p_c^0)]\tau(y)$ from a strict price fall $p_c^1<p_c^0$, and under quasilinearity with the price of $S$ fixed this is its exact equivalent variation. Because $w(p_c^1)-w(p_c^0)>0$, this gain relative to income is non-increasing in $y$ if and only if $\tau(y)/y$ is non-increasing."))

# ---- Proposition 2
R.append((r"In Regime~H, dispersed holders receive $w(p_c)\tau(y)$, which rises as $p_c$ falls. In Regime~C, with a single owner of the codified asset and Bertrand competition, the price of $t$-services is $P_C=\min\{w(p_c),\max\{r_h,k_t\}\}$. Holders' premium over their opportunity cost is $\max\{0,\min\{w(p_c),k_t\}-r_h\}\le k_t$ per unit, and it does not rise as $p_c$ falls once $w(p_c)\ge k_t$. If $k_t<r_h$, holders are undercut, the owner earns $\min\{w(p_c),r_h\}-k_t$ per unit, and the remainder $w(p_c)-P_C$ accrues to users of $t$-services. As $k_t\to0$, holders' premium vanishes whatever $p_c$ is.",
r"""In Regime~H, dispersed holders receive $w(p_c)\tau(y)$, which rises as $p_c$ falls. In Regime~C, let the holders and a single owner of the codified asset compete in prices (Bertrand) to supply $t$-services that users value at $w(p_c)$ per unit.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),itemsep=0pt]
\item If $\min\{r_h,k_t\}>w(p_c)$, no $t$-services are traded.
\item If $\min\{r_h,k_t\}\le w(p_c)$, the price is $P_C=\min\{w(p_c),\max\{r_h,k_t\}\}$. If $r_h\le k_t$, holders supply, and their premium over opportunity cost is $\min\{w(p_c),k_t\}-r_h\le k_t$ per unit. If $k_t<r_h$, the owner supplies and earns $\min\{w(p_c),r_h\}-k_t$ per unit, and holders earn no premium.
\item In either case holders' premium does not rise as $p_c$ falls once $w(p_c)\ge k_t$, and it vanishes as $k_t\to0$, whatever $p_c$ is.
\end{enumerate}"""))

# ---- Proposition 3
R.append((r"\item \emph{Verification rising with income, no bias.} At any income at which households buy,",
r"\item \emph{Verification rising with income, no bias.} Let $v$ be continuous and piecewise $C^1$ with $v'\ge0$, so that $p_e(y)$ is continuous, non-increasing and piecewise $C^1$, as Lemma~\ref{lem:schedule} requires. On any interval of incomes at which all households buy,"))
R.append((r"\item \emph{Overconfidence.} For an entrant $y$ with $\theta(v(y))<1$, every posted price in the non-empty interval $\big(R(y)-\pi(y)L,\,R(y)-\theta\pi(y)L\big]$ leads the household to buy, and leaves it strictly worse off than without AI. The interval's length $(1-\theta)\pi L$ is decreasing in $v$.",
r"\item \emph{Overconfidence.} For an entrant $y$ with $\theta(v(y))<1$ and $\pi(y)=e(1-v(y))>0$, every posted price in the non-empty interval $\big(R(y)-\pi(y)L,\,R(y)-\theta\pi(y)L\big]$ leads the household to buy, and leaves it strictly worse off than without AI. The interval contains admissible prices $p\ge0$ if and only if $R(y)\ge\theta\pi(y)L$; its admissible part is $\big(\max\{0,R(y)-\pi(y)L\},\,R(y)-\theta\pi(y)L\big]$. The interval's length $(1-\theta)\pi L$ is non-increasing in $v$."))
R.append((r"\item \emph{Intermediated verification.} An intermediary verifies with quality $v_I$, incurs a fixed cost $F$ and marginal cost $m$ per client, and serves $n$ clients at the zero-profit fee $q=m+F/n$. Relative gains are non-increasing over the whole distribution if three conditions hold: (d1)~every household for which intermediation is efficient, $q\le eL(v_I-v)$, is served through it; (d2)~$p+m+F/n+e(1-v_I)L\le(1-\eta)R(y_L)$, equivalently",
r"\item \emph{Intermediated verification.} An intermediary verifies with quality $v_I$, incurs a fixed cost $F$ and marginal cost $m$ per client, and serves $n$ clients at the zero-profit fee $q=m+F/n$. A household routed to it faces the effective price $p_I\equiv p+q+e(1-v_I)L$. Suppose the lowest-income household is rationed before AI, $R(y_L)\le P_0$, and the denominator of $n^*$ below is positive. Relative gains are non-increasing over the whole distribution if three conditions hold together: (d1)~every household for which intermediation is efficient, $q\le eL(v_I-v)$, is served through it; (d2)~$p_I\le(1-\eta)R(y_L)$, that is $p+m+F/n+e(1-v_I)L\le(1-\eta)R(y_L)$, equivalently"))
R.append((r"\item \emph{Under-demand under overconfidence.} A household that scales its own error rate by $\theta$ buys intermediation only if $q\le eL[\theta(1-v)-(1-v_I)]$. Whenever $\theta<1$, there are households for whom intermediation is efficient but not bought. Condition (d1) can therefore fail in a voluntary market. It is restored if intermediation is the default channel.",
r"\item \emph{Under-demand under overconfidence.} Intermediation is efficient for household $y$ if and only if $q\le b(y)\equiv eL(v_I-v)$. A household that scales its own error rate by $\theta$ buys it only if $q\le\hat b(y)\equiv eL[\theta(1-v)-(1-v_I)]$, and $\hat b(y)<b(y)$ whenever $\theta<1$ and $v<1$. Households for which intermediation is efficient but not bought exist if and only if $\hat b(y)<q\le b(y)$ for some household. Two sufficient conditions are: (e1)~the fee lies between some household's perceived and true benefit; or (e2)~$q=0$ and some household has $v(y)<v_I$ and $\theta(v)(1-v)<1-v_I$, which holds for $v$ close enough to $v_I$ whenever $\theta(v_I)<1$. When such households exist, (d1) fails in a voluntary market. It holds if intermediation is the default channel for every household with $q\le b(y)$."))
R.append((r"Parts~(b)--(c) are the regressive cases. Parts~(d)--(e) state the condition on intermediary cost and density that restores progressivity, and explain why a voluntary market for verification does not suffice under overconfidence.",
r"Parts~(b)--(c) are the regressive cases. Part~(d) states the joint conditions (routing, density and the residual gradient) under which intermediation restores progressivity. Part~(e) states when a voluntary market for verification falls short under overconfidence. It is an existence result: with $q=0$ and verification capacities far below $v_I$, every household for which intermediation is efficient buys it."))

# ---- Proposition 4
R.append((r"Each human handles one opportunity per period. Compute capacity exceeds $H$.",
r"Each human handles one opportunity per period. Compute capacity $\mu$ exceeds $H$, so it can staff every human-assisted opportunity and leave capacity for opportunities completed by AI alone."))
R.append((r"\item With non-autonomous AI, $r^*=0$ and $w_j=\max\{s_j-C(D),0\}$. A higher density $D$ raises every employed wage by $c_1/D^2$, expands employment, and compresses wage ratios. A higher $z_A$ raises all wages, and raises low-knowledge wages most.",
r"\item With non-autonomous AI, $r^*=0$ and $w_j=\max\{s_j-C(D),0\}$. A higher density $D$ raises the wage of every holder who remains employed by $c_1/D^2$ (a local statement: at the employment threshold the wage schedule has a kink), expands employment, and compresses wage ratios among the employed. A higher $z_A$ raises the wage of employed holder $j$ by $(1-z_j)$ per unit of $z_A$: all employed wages rise, low-knowledge wages most, and the change differs across holders."))

# ---- Proposition 5
R.append((r"After a small cost decline $dk<0$, households gain $\rho Q|dk|$, the supplier gains $Q|dk|$, and domestic welfare rises by $[\rho+(1-f)]Q|dk|$. The progressivity condition (d2) is relaxed by $\rho|dk|$. An access fee $A$ charged to intermediaries raises the density threshold to $n^*(A)=(F+A)/[(1-\eta)R(y_L)-p-m-e(1-v_I)L]$, so low-density catchments lose the guarantee of Proposition~\ref{prop:verification}(d).",
r"""After a small cost decline $dk<0$, to first order in $dk$ (terms of order $o(|dk|)$ omitted), households gain $\rho Q|dk|$, the supplier gains $Q|dk|$, and domestic welfare rises by $[\rho+(1-f)]Q|dk|$. The progressivity condition (d2) is relaxed by $\rho|dk|$. Let $A$ be an access fee charged to each intermediary as a fixed amount per period, not per client. The intermediary's zero-profit fee becomes $q_A=m+(F+A)/n$, and (d2) becomes
\[
\text{(d2}_A\text{)}\qquad p+m+\frac{F+A}{n}+e(1-v_I)L\le(1-\eta)R(y_L),\quad\text{equivalently}\quad n\ \ge\ n^*(A)\equiv\frac{F+A}{(1-\eta)R(y_L)-p-m-e(1-v_I)L},
\]
so low-density catchments lose the guarantee of Proposition~\ref{prop:verification}(d)."""))

